yoyo model <unknown> and yoyo skill show <missing> now exit nonzero, and the change is committed as 838b7e3c. Build, all tests, clippy with -D warnings and the format check all passed.

I ran the debug binary from /tmp with stdin at /dev/null, before and after:

| command | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| yoyo model nosuch-model-x | 0 | 2 (usage error) |
| yoyo skill show nosuch | 0 | 1 |
| yoyo model list | 0 | 0 |
| yoyo skill show demo --skills <tmpdir> | 0 | 0, output byte-identical (cmp) |

What changed:
• **Model:** `ModelSubcommand::exit_code()` returns 2 for an unrecognised subcommand and 0 for the valid ones. The command prints what it always printed, then exits with that code.
• **Skill:** a new `handle_skill_status` returns 1 when `show` can't find the named skill. It also returns 1 if the skill's file can't be read, which is slightly more than the task asked for. The in-session /skill command calls the same code and ignores the status, so it behaves as before. The output is now built as text first (skill_show_report), so a test can check a successful show prints exactly what it used to.
• **Shared exit path:** I added `exit_if_nonzero(code)` next to the existing `exit_if_failed` from 6d84deff, and `exit_if_failed` now calls it. There is still only one place that exits the process, and the code is never worked out by reading output text.
• **Tests:** the existing model-parsing test table now also checks the exit code (2 only for unknown subcommands, 0 otherwise). A new skill test uses a skill in a temp directory and checks both cases: found gives 0 with the exact output, missing gives 1. Neither test exits the process, so the test binary is safe.
• **Docs:** docs/src/usage/commands.md already described exit codes for test and run, so I added these two there.
• **Size limit:** `src/dispatch_sub.rs` was already over the line cap and on the exceptions list. It grew from 2164 to 2193 lines, within the allowed drift, and I updated its entry in tests/module_size.rs with a note.

The find/def no-match question, still an open decision in #982, and the other return Some(None) branches were not touched.
